ves  and then step number three is look to  help them progress so you're wearing  their skin  you've learned about their city you're  doing regular you know non-operational  check-ins and you've learned about them  and what they care about  now how can you help sometimes it may  mean you have to educate yourself  so for example there was a person on my  team who had lyme disease i'd never  heard of what lyme disease was  never knew what it was never heard about  it didn't care i'm ignorant about most  things  but the fact that he had it now i went  to google news and i created a news  alert around lando seens  and when something came out about lyme  disease that was interesting new novel  some new study or some new celebrity  came out shania twain has it avril  lavigne has it justin bieber has it  i would share it with that person and it  made made them feel  like i cared and i did care right i'm  not everyday going  lyme disease lyme disease lyme disease  right but the fact that it  was in my news alerts and i was thinking  about that person made them feel special  so it has nothing to do with my business  right i'm always looking for  opportunities to help somebody grow and  learn what skills that you want to pick  up  but outside of that what do they care  about what are they struggling with and  how can you be a potential resource  and the more it's even outside of what  you're supposed to do as a boss  the better it is even for that  relationship so look for ways to help  them progress  look for people who step up it's not  about their resume  it's not about where they used to work  it's not about how good their references  are  it's about are they willing to do the  work when other people aren't  and it's up to you to give them the  opportunity to step  up and shine and so i think about one of  the businesses that i run toronto dance  salsa largest salsa dance school maybe  north america  and when i first took it over the  original owner had to leave the business  because her back was hurting  and she couldn't dance anymore it was  causing her  severe physical harm and there was one  guy named alex  who now helps run the business who was  her assistant who was training with her  and i knew alex and i didn't think alex  you know would do a good job as an  instructor i was actually against him  even training to be an instructor  because he wasn't committed he wasn't  following through he wasn't taking  feedback most importantly  and here he was training with her  and when she came down with the injury i  was the only person who could actually  go and teach  that class as at least a short-term gap  and so  alex became super deflated because  a guy can't dance with another guy i  mean i guess you could but in our motto  it's usually  a man and woman at the front so if i'm  coming in to teach the class  then i would have a female assistant  and so alice is really bummed one  because the origin owner is injured and  now two the thing he worked so hard for  was being taken away from him  so he comes to pick me up from my place  we're driving over to toronto dance elsa  and i say hey i've got we got two  two choices here one  i'll teach and you can watch i'll still  train you  because she was training you and i'll  honor that commitment and you need to  show up for your training and make sure  you're doing your part  so i could teach the class and you could  sit on the sidelines  and i'll train you or two you take over  the class  now it's your class and we'll give you a  female assistant  and i'll train you from the sidelines  one do you want and he didn't know the  material there's so many reasons why i  wouldn't work out he's got three hours  of teaching he had to do that night that  he wasn't prepared to do  the entire class for and he was already  going in expecting that  he was gonna be benched and he said i'll  do it  and it's in these moments that this is  when i recognize  great people from my team like when  you're not ready  but you're given an opportunity do you  step up he wouldn't have been given that  opportunity by most people  he wasn't ready he made tons of mistakes  he had to teach the man  and the women's part and he barely knew  the women's part  and i'm not so concerned about did he  get it perfect what i'm concerned about  is did he try  is he saying yes and i love throwing  people into opportunities  and seeing if they have that in them to  say  i'll do it i'll do my best i'll try  those are the people you want to  surround yourself with now fast forward  to where we are now  it's something i have to remind alex of  when he's training new people  he's bringing new people on and now he's  built himself up to having such a high  standard for what a class looks like  that he doesn't let people move on until  they've  hit almost mastery so you need to lower  the bar  you need to give people opportunities  you need to give them a taste you need  to see what they're made of see if  they're capable  do you remember how bad you were when  you first started  i have to remind him about the process  about how  there was one move that he didn't want  to teach at all didn't feel comfortable  was word that he would fall and fall  down on his face while while doing it  turning twice and he said can you teach  this evan like nope you're teaching it  if you fall you fall and he went up and  he taught it and he didn't fall  and so reminding him now that he needs  to treat  the people on his team who are under him  now that he's bees built up  and give them opportunities and not hold  them back because they're not perfect  you want to find the people who are  willing to step up but you can't just  blame them  are you giving people the opportunities  are you allowing them  to shine are you giving them some rope  to play with and not hanging them  if they make a mistake so yes it's about  finding people but it's two  also about you owning your role to say  are you giving your team the opportunity  to step  up and impress not just you but impress  themselves as well  okay how do we do it how do we build a  better team how do we build a better  culture how do we get people to step  up three steps let's go step number one  is fixing your hiring process most  people have a broken hiring process  most people hire people based off of  the resume the cover letter an interview  and references that's typically  the process right most of those things  don't test for what they're actually  going to be doing  you're putting people through this  machine that is totally unrelated to the  work they're actually going to be doing  if you're trying to hire a videographer  for example  you're trying to hire an editor for  example for a youtube channel  what their resume looks like doesn't  matter what their cover looks like  doesn't matter like their ability to  write  a cover letter doesn't matter you're  hiring an editor i don't care how  how well you write if you're shakespeare  in writing but you suck at editing  it doesn't matter references are almost  always bs  and interviews as well almost always  don't work  because again somebody could be the most  introverted shy  person of all time and suck in an  interview  but be great as an editor so most of the  time i think the hiring process is  broken and people are  are not judging people correctly the  best way to do it  i think is to actually work on projects  together so what we'll do is when i'm  doing a job post  it'll actually be one big ugly paragraph  and somewhere in the middle  we'll ask some kind of skill testing  question like tell me how many youtube  subscribers i have  and put that in the first line of your  application eighty percent of the people  who apply for a job  don't even catch that question never  find my youtube channel and don't put it  in the first line of  their application so if you're not gonna  spend time actually reading  my job ad why am i going to spend time  reading your application this is why  it's broken  it takes them eight seconds to click  apply  and they're just using the same cover  letter resume everything they've done  for everybody else  but it takes you it takes you time to  read every one of them part of why  we hate the hiring process because it  takes so long that then if you finally  get somebody through this broken process  you don't want to lose them and you keep  somebody who's just  okay because you want to go through that  painful process all over again  if they don't respond to the skill  testing question right away  you already lose 80 of the people now  you've got 20 to work with and what i  love doing next  is having a trial job so instead of  interviewing or anything else  we're going to work on a project  together i'll give up the five hours on  a project  go edit this video let's see what you  got and in the process of working with  them  it actually maps really well to the work  they're going to be doing if they  actually got the job  and we see how well do they work  together how quickly are they working do  they ask smart questions  are they delivering a quality product  and in that in that paid  trial it's a paid trial in a patreon we  figure out who we end up wanting to work  with and it's often  it's often the people who don't look  great on paper a lot of the people on my  team  don't look that great on paper but  they're great  they're great people and so fix your  hiring process and in your selection  process  you're testing for the things that you  actually care about that map well  to the job that they're gonna have  because most people's hiring process  is dead broken step number two is give  them opportunities  so when you have people it's like great  what else are you working on building  growing learning what do they want to  get into one of the advantages of being  an entrepreneur is  there's so many things so many ideas we  have so many places we want to grow so  many  new things we want to do 